1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,212
A developer finishes a small change. It passes every test.

2
00:00:04,762 --> 00:00:05,505
In one team,

3
00:00:05,505 --> 00:00:07,983
it reaches customers the same afternoon.

4
00:00:07,983 --> 00:00:08,974
In another team,

5
00:00:08,974 --> 00:00:10,274
with the same change,

6
00:00:10,274 --> 00:00:12,442
it waits three weeks for a meeting.

7
00:00:12,992 --> 00:00:17,835
The difference is not the code. It is how far the pipeline is allowed to go, on its own.

8
00:00:18,385 --> 00:00:19,025
Today: CI,

9
00:00:19,025 --> 00:00:20,305
continuous delivery,

10
00:00:20,305 --> 00:00:21,969
and continuous deployment.

11
00:00:21,969 --> 00:00:24,017
Three names that sound the same,

12
00:00:24,017 --> 00:00:26,065
and mean three different things.

13
00:00:27,615 --> 00:00:29,044
Here is what to expect.

14
00:00:29,594 --> 00:00:32,645
One: continuous integration, and what it really checks.

15
00:00:33,195 --> 00:00:36,566
Two: continuous delivery, where a human presses the button.

16
00:00:37,116 --> 00:00:39,974
Three: continuous deployment, where nobody does.

17
00:00:40,524 --> 00:00:42,525
Four: a live demo of all three,

18
00:00:42,525 --> 00:00:44,460
with one real pipeline script.

19
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:46,267
Then we break it on purpose,

20
00:00:46,267 --> 00:00:48,396
and see what protects production.

21
00:00:48,946 --> 00:00:52,168
This is lesson two of the DevOps fundamentals domain.

22
00:00:54,218 --> 00:00:56,927
Start with continuous integration. CI.

23
00:00:57,477 --> 00:01:02,235
Everyone merges their work into one shared branch, often. At least once a day.

24
00:01:02,785 --> 00:01:06,646
Every merge starts an automatic build, and an automatic test run.

25
00:01:07,196 --> 00:01:08,244
If something breaks,

26
00:01:08,244 --> 00:01:09,659
the team learns in minutes,

27
00:01:09,659 --> 00:01:12,017
while the change is still fresh in your mind.

28
00:01:12,567 --> 00:01:14,273
Notice what CI does not do.

29
00:01:14,273 --> 00:01:16,988
It does not put anything in front of users.

30
00:01:16,988 --> 00:01:22,167
It only answers one question: does this change work together with everyone else's?

31
00:01:23,717 --> 00:01:26,512
Next, continuous delivery. The first CD.

32
00:01:27,062 --> 00:01:28,690
It starts where CI stops.

33
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:30,058
After the tests pass,

34
00:01:30,058 --> 00:01:32,207
the pipeline packages the change,

35
00:01:32,207 --> 00:01:33,184
into a release,

36
00:01:33,184 --> 00:01:34,422
and keeps it ready.

37
00:01:34,972 --> 00:01:39,345
At any moment, the latest version could go live. It is always deployable.

38
00:01:39,895 --> 00:01:41,490
But a person decides when.

39
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:42,840
Maybe a product owner,

40
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:43,822
maybe a manager,

41
00:01:43,822 --> 00:01:45,478
maybe the on-call engineer.

42
00:01:45,478 --> 00:01:46,828
They press the button.

43
00:01:47,378 --> 00:01:52,136
That button is called a gate, or an approval. It is a choice, and it is a valid one.

44
00:01:53,686 --> 00:01:56,481
Then continuous deployment. The second CD.

45
00:01:57,031 --> 00:01:57,915
Same pipeline,

46
00:01:57,915 --> 00:01:59,367
but without the button.

47
00:01:59,367 --> 00:02:00,756
If every check passes,

48
00:02:00,756 --> 00:02:03,281
the change goes to production by itself.

49
00:02:03,831 --> 00:02:07,543
No ticket, no meeting. A commit can reach users in minutes.

50
00:02:08,093 --> 00:02:09,132
That sounds risky.

51
00:02:09,132 --> 00:02:09,709
And it is,

52
00:02:09,709 --> 00:02:10,748
unless your tests,

53
00:02:10,748 --> 00:02:11,902
and your monitoring,

54
00:02:11,902 --> 00:02:14,152
are strong enough to replace the human.

55
00:02:14,702 --> 00:02:19,182
So the tests become the gate. The pipeline is the one that says yes, or no.

56
00:02:20,732 --> 00:02:24,935
Let us watch the three side by side. One change, three pipelines.

57
00:02:25,485 --> 00:02:30,775
The first stops after the tests. That is CI. The change is integrated, and proven.

58
00:02:31,325 --> 00:02:32,770
The second goes further,

59
00:02:32,770 --> 00:02:33,734
and is packaged.

60
00:02:33,734 --> 00:02:35,299
Then it waits at the gate,

61
00:02:35,299 --> 00:02:36,082
for a person.

62
00:02:36,082 --> 00:02:37,768
That is continuous delivery.

63
00:02:38,318 --> 00:02:39,596
The third has no gate.

64
00:02:39,596 --> 00:02:41,802
It goes straight through to the users.

65
00:02:41,802 --> 00:02:43,545
That is continuous deployment.

66
00:02:44,095 --> 00:02:48,191
Same start, three finishing lines. Each one includes the one before.

67
00:02:49,741 --> 00:02:52,621
Before you remove the gate, five things must be true.

68
00:02:53,171 --> 00:02:58,099
One: strong automatic tests. If the tests miss a bug, nobody else will catch it.

69
00:02:58,649 --> 00:03:03,940
Two: small changes. A small change is easy to understand, and easy to undo.

70
00:03:04,490 --> 00:03:08,650
Three: feature flags. You can ship the code, and switch the feature on later.

71
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:13,402
Four: fast rollback. One command, and the last good version is back.

72
00:03:13,952 --> 00:03:18,752
Five: monitoring and alerts, so a bad release is noticed in minutes, not days.

73
00:03:20,302 --> 00:03:23,758
So which one should you choose? It depends on the cost of a mistake.

74
00:03:24,308 --> 00:03:25,292
A busy web shop,

75
00:03:25,292 --> 00:03:26,276
like CloudCart,

76
00:03:26,276 --> 00:03:28,429
with good tests and quick rollback,

77
00:03:28,429 --> 00:03:31,135
can go all the way to continuous deployment.

78
00:03:31,685 --> 00:03:32,140
A bank,

79
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:33,442
or a medical device,

80
00:03:33,442 --> 00:03:35,329
often keeps a human approval.

81
00:03:35,329 --> 00:03:37,151
That is continuous delivery,

82
00:03:37,151 --> 00:03:38,127
and it is fine.

83
00:03:38,677 --> 00:03:43,349
And every team, in every case, starts with CI. Without it, nothing else works.

84
00:03:43,899 --> 00:03:48,166
Many teams use both: automatic to staging, and a button for production.

85
00:03:49,716 --> 00:03:53,983
Go to the demo folder.

86
00:03:55,533 --> 00:03:57,431
Look around.

87
00:03:57,981 --> 00:04:02,440
A script, pipeline.sh, and a folder with the shop code and its check.

88
00:04:02,990 --> 00:04:07,321
The code.

89
00:04:07,871 --> 00:04:10,943
One function. It multiplies a price by a quantity.

90
00:04:11,493 --> 00:04:15,653
CI.

91
00:04:16,203 --> 00:04:16,606
Build,

92
00:04:16,606 --> 00:04:16,943
test,

93
00:04:16,943 --> 00:04:17,481
package,

94
00:04:17,481 --> 00:04:17,817
done.

95
00:04:17,817 --> 00:04:19,028
Two checks passed.

96
00:04:19,028 --> 00:04:21,449
The change is integrated and tested.

97
00:04:21,449 --> 00:04:22,795
Nothing is deployed.

98
00:04:23,345 --> 00:04:28,251
Delivery.

99
00:04:28,801 --> 00:04:33,473
The same steps, then ready. It stops. Deployable, and waiting for a human.

100
00:04:34,023 --> 00:04:36,797
A human approves.

101
00:04:38,147 --> 00:04:40,664
Deploy. Production now runs release one.

102
00:04:41,214 --> 00:04:46,547
Deployment.

103
00:04:47,097 --> 00:04:50,895
No gate. The tests passed, so release two went live, by itself.

104
00:04:51,445 --> 00:04:56,522
Break it.

105
00:04:57,072 --> 00:04:59,653
Run it again.

106
00:05:00,703 --> 00:05:01,586
The tests fail,

107
00:05:01,586 --> 00:05:03,940
and the pipeline says: nothing released.

108
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:05,883
The bug never reached production.

109
00:05:05,883 --> 00:05:07,295
The tests were the gate.

110
00:05:07,845 --> 00:05:10,427
Check production.

111
00:05:10,977 --> 00:05:15,542
Still two releases. The broken change was stopped, with no human involved.

112
00:05:16,092 --> 00:05:19,633
Write it down.

113
00:05:28,583 --> 00:05:30,653
Three short lines, in your own words.

114
00:05:31,203 --> 00:05:33,656
Check the file.

115
00:05:35,206 --> 00:05:38,427
Your practice task: run one pipeline in three modes.

116
00:05:38,977 --> 00:05:41,495
One: run CI, and read the four steps.

117
00:05:42,045 --> 00:05:46,013
Two: run delivery, and see it stop. Then run it again with approve.

118
00:05:46,563 --> 00:05:50,680
Three: run deployment. Check that production now holds two releases.

119
00:05:51,230 --> 00:05:52,538
Four: break the code,

120
00:05:52,538 --> 00:05:53,845
run deployment again,

121
00:05:53,845 --> 00:05:56,398
and confirm that production is untouched.

122
00:05:56,398 --> 00:05:58,142
Then write your three lines.

123
00:06:00,192 --> 00:06:03,413
Where to find the demo files. Pause here, and write it down.

124
00:06:03,963 --> 00:06:09,638
One: the repository, github.com/it-waves/devops-ai.

125
00:06:10,188 --> 00:06:14,369
Two: the folder academy/lesson146-demo.

126
00:06:14,919 --> 00:06:20,103
Three: the script pipeline.sh, the source folder, and a read me with the answers.

127
00:06:20,653 --> 00:06:22,341
Four: clone the repository,

128
00:06:22,341 --> 00:06:23,591
and copy the folder.

129
00:06:23,591 --> 00:06:25,716
You need a shell and python three.

130
00:06:25,716 --> 00:06:26,904
Nothing to install.

131
00:06:27,454 --> 00:06:28,220
Five: again,

132
00:06:28,220 --> 00:06:29,624
no lab in this domain.

133
00:06:29,624 --> 00:06:31,667
It is about ideas and decisions,

134
00:06:31,667 --> 00:06:33,263
so the practice is plain,

135
00:06:33,263 --> 00:06:35,753
and the answers are on the next screen.

136
00:06:37,803 --> 00:06:40,278
Check yourself. Six points. Tick each one.

137
00:06:40,828 --> 00:06:43,110
One: CI builds and tests, and stops.

138
00:06:43,660 --> 00:06:46,519
Two: delivery stops at the gate, and says ready.

139
00:06:47,069 --> 00:06:48,797
Three: approve releases it.

140
00:06:49,347 --> 00:06:51,566
Four: deployment releases by itself.

141
00:06:52,116 --> 00:06:54,462
Five: with the bug, nothing is released.

142
00:06:55,012 --> 00:06:59,322
Six: production still holds two releases, and your file has three lines.

143
00:06:59,872 --> 00:07:01,728
All six, and you know the difference.

144
00:07:03,778 --> 00:07:05,549
Your turn, on your own machine.

145
00:07:06,099 --> 00:07:07,933
One: copy the demo folder.

146
00:07:08,483 --> 00:07:10,382
Two: run the three modes, in order.

147
00:07:10,932 --> 00:07:13,599
Three: break the code, and run deployment again.

148
00:07:14,149 --> 00:07:15,727
Four: write your three lines.

149
00:07:16,277 --> 00:07:17,803
Five: tick the six points.

150
00:07:17,803 --> 00:07:22,144
Then think about your own team: which of the three is your pipeline today?

151
00:07:22,694 --> 00:07:24,529
Pause the video, and begin now.

152
00:07:40,079 --> 00:07:40,954
Quick check.

153
00:07:41,504 --> 00:07:42,698
What does CI check?

154
00:07:43,248 --> 00:07:45,894
What is the difference between delivery and deployment?

155
00:07:46,444 --> 00:07:49,430
Name three things you need before removing the gate.

156
00:07:49,980 --> 00:07:51,964
Which one starts every team's journey?

157
00:07:52,514 --> 00:07:54,814
Objective covered: MC16,

158
00:07:54,814 --> 00:07:57,039
objective two: distinguish CI,

159
00:07:57,039 --> 00:07:58,522
continuous delivery,

160
00:07:58,522 --> 00:08:00,450
and continuous deployment.

161
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,070
